MARS modern-kernel QEMU tests
============================

Run from the repository root without root privileges. Substitute installed
kernel releases, each with /lib/modules/<release>/{build,vmlinuz} and its
module tree. The configured build tree must include Module.symvers.

    python3 test_suite/qemu/run.py 7.2.9-2-cachyos
    python3 test_suite/qemu/run.py 6.18.55-2-cachyos-lts --logical-block-size 4096
    python3 test_suite/qemu/run.py 7.3.0-rc6-2-cachyos-rc --no-discard
    MARS_DEBUG=y MARS_DEBUG_MEM=y python3 test_suite/qemu/run.py 7.2.9-2-cachyos
    python3 test_suite/qemu/replicate.py 7.2.9-2-cachyos 7.3.0-rc6-2-cachyos-rc
    python3 test_suite/qemu/replicate.py 7.3.0-rc6-2-cachyos-rc 7.1.5-arch1-2
    python3 test_suite/qemu/crash.py 7.2.9-2-cachyos

Host prerequisites: x86-64 Linux, Python 3, QEMU with microvm and KVM,
make, matching compiler/binutils or LLVM, Perl/core modules, cpio, gzip,
kmod, e2fsprogs, iproute2, util-linux (including blkdiscard/fstrim), GNU
coreutils, bash, grep, sed, awk, findutils and pstree. Fault/crash tests also
need dmsetup. WireGuard tests need wg. These tools are copied into the
initramfs; no host module installation, TAP, sudo or host block disk is used.
The runner selects LLVM=1 for kernels configured with Clang.

The target kernel needs modules, initramfs/gzip, devtmpfs, procfs, sysfs,
tracefs, ext4, virtio-mmio/block/net, IPv4/TCP, sysctl and MARS dependencies.
Additional test modules and binaries can be included using repeatable
--extra-module and --extra-binary arguments. Configure prepared CI kernels
with test_suite/ci/configure-kernel.sh. The single-node runner supports
--accel tcg without KVM; allow longer timeouts for software emulation.

Each guest has 2 vCPUs, 2 GiB RAM, a sparse 32 GiB metadata image and a
128 MiB data image. Images live under /var/tmp by default; TMPDIR overrides
that location. A RAM-backed /tmp can fill after a few runs. --workdir accepts
a new directory. Logs, sources, initramfs and disk images are retained at
the printed path; remove them after inspection to reclaim space.
--prepare-only builds artifacts without booting QEMU. --module-source uses
an alternate module source tree while retaining the current guest tools.

Single-node coverage includes module load, resource creation, capacity and
logical/physical sector sizes, durable writes/direct reads, discard and
WRITE_ZEROES, overlapping rewrites, adjacent-data preservation, invalid
ranges, native discard or fallback trace events, demotion/detach/unload,
backing-device comparison, reload, ext4, file fsync and fstrim. Readback of
native discarded sectors is checked against QEMU's zero-returning backend;
this is not a general promise about reads following DISCARD.

Two-node tests use a private QEMU socket Ethernet link. They bootstrap UUID
and node IP records, then exercise native metadata exchange, resource join,
full initial sync, log shipping, discard/overwrite/WRITE_ZEROES and direct
secondary backing verification. The isolated guests explicitly enable
mars_allow_insecure; deployed networking should use WireGuard. To verify
old-reader fallback with a separate pre-change checkout/archive:

    python3 test_suite/qemu/replicate.py PRIMARY_KERNEL SECONDARY_KERNEL \
        --secondary-source /path/to/pre-change-source

The pre-change module uses its legacy parameters on the secondary. Commit
87ac5140 was tested this way against the new primary: protocol negotiation
and zero-write replay of discard records passed. This is a rolling-upgrade
compatibility check, not a claim that the old module has the new fixes.

crash.py gives the data backend a 60-second dm-delay while logs use a
separate device. It waits for write/discard/overwrite acknowledgment,
asserts the backing data is still stale, SIGKILLs QEMU, then reboots the
same images with an undelayed backend. Recovery must reproduce the expected
bytes both through MARS and on the backing device after detach/unload.
There is no guest sync or graceful shutdown at the crash point. This tests
guest crash recovery, not loss of the host's physical drive cache.

Storage and protocol failure fixtures:

    python3 test_suite/qemu/run.py KERNEL --guest-script test_suite/qemu/faults.sh \
        --extra-module dm_mod --extra-binary dmsetup
    python3 test_suite/qemu/run.py KERNEL --guest-script test_suite/qemu/faults.sh \
        --extra-module dm_mod --extra-binary dmsetup --append mars_fault=log
    python3 test_suite/qemu/run.py KERNEL --guest-script test_suite/qemu/network.sh
    python3 test_suite/qemu/run.py KERNEL --guest-script test_suite/qemu/wireguard.sh \
        --extra-module wireguard --extra-binary wg

The dm-error fixtures verify first-error latching, failed subsequent writes,
preserved replay positions and unload after data/log failures. The network
fixture sends malformed descriptors, strings, paths and callbacks, tests
export policy, IPv6 and reloads. WireGuard creates a peer network namespace
inside the guest, exchanges encrypted commands in both directions, checks
source binding, and rejects a local plaintext bypass. It does not exercise
two complete resource replicas over WireGuard.

Runners fail on timeouts, missing markers, guest failures and kernel
warnings/oops/panics. Pair guests are stopped after verification; graceful
teardown is covered separately. See docu/modern-kernel.rst for dated results
and limits. Existing compiler warnings, sustained-load testing, KASAN/KCSAN
runtime coverage and signature-enforced loading remain release checks.
